      "Verse 15.7 sits in the fifteenth chapter of the Gita, known as Purushottama Yoga, which begins with the image of an upside-down tree of worldly existence and moves on to describe three kinds of purusha: the perishable, the imperishable and the highest. This verse speaks about the individual living being, the jiva, and says that it is an amsha, a portion or fragment, of the Lord himself, and that it is eternal, sanatana.",
